\subsection{Forward Coupling: Simulation to Spacecraft}
UUAS provides pre-launch constraints for spacecraft configuration:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Trajectory Design:} MHD-Q1 dynamo geometry determines
    optimal orbital planes and periapsis altitudes for magnetic field
    sampling.
    \item \textbf{Probe Descent Profile:} THERMO-Q2 phase diagrams define
    entry corridor, parachute deployment altitude, and sampling cadence.
    \item \textbf{Instrument Tuning:} ION-Q4 predicts H$_3^+$ emission
    strengths, setting spectrometer bandpass and integration times.
\end{itemize}

Thus, spacecraft architecture is derived from simulation physics.

\subsection{Reverse Coupling: Spacecraft to Simulation}
Telemetry from UAP-01 provides real-time validation for UUAS:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Magnetometer Data:} Field vectors update multipole
    coefficients and dynamo layer thickness.
    \item \textbf{Probe Descent Data:} Methane fractions, temperature
    gradients, and aerosol densities refine deep-mantle EOS and diamond
    nucleation thresholds.
    \item \textbf{Auroral Spectra:} H$_3^+$ emission profiles constrain
    plasma transport and radiative cooling terms.
    \item \textbf{Encrypted Telemetry:} FHE-Q3 validates homomorphic
    lattice encryption under real mission conditions.
\end{itemize}

The spacecraft becomes a dynamic boundary condition for UUAS.

\subsection{Mathematical Coupling Framework}
The coupling loop is formalized as:
\[
\mathcal{S}_{\text{craft}}(t) \xrightarrow{\text{telemetry}}
\mathcal{U}_{\text{sim}}(t)
\xrightarrow{\text{model update}}
\mathcal{S}_{\text{craft}}(t+\Delta t),
\]
where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\mathcal{S}_{\text{craft}}$ is spacecraft state and
    measurement vector,
    \item $\mathcal{U}_{\text{sim}}$ is UUAS multi-scale physics state,
    \item $\Delta t$ is the update interval.
\end{itemize}

UUAS solves:
\[
\mathcal{U}_{\text{sim}} =
\left\{
\text{DFTB},\;
\text{EOS}_{\text{mantle}},\;
\text{MHD},\;
\text{Plasma},\;
\text{FHE}
\right\},
\]
and returns updated sampling geometry and instrument parameters.

\subsection{Encrypted Coupling Layer}
All telemetry is processed through homomorphic lattice encryption:
\[
\mathcal{E}(\mathbf{d}) =
\text{FHE}(\mathbf{d}),
\]
where $\mathbf{d}$ is raw spacecraft data.

UUAS operates directly on $\mathcal{E}(\mathbf{d})$ without decryption,
ensuring:
\begin{itemize}
    \item zero leakage,
    \item zero bit-level errors,
    \item quantum-resistant computation,
    \item secure multi-node solver execution.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Closed-Loop Science Control}
The spacecraft–simulation system forms a control-theoretic loop:
\[
\mathbf{y}(t) = \mathcal{S}_{\text{craft}}(t),
\qquad
\mathbf{u}(t) = \mathcal{U}_{\text{sim}}(t),
\]
with feedback:
\[
\mathbf{u}(t) = f(\mathbf{y}(t)),
\]
and forward propagation:
\[
\mathbf{y}(t+\Delta t) = g(\mathbf{u}(t)).
\]

This architecture ensures continuous co-evolution of spacecraft sampling
and simulation fidelity.

\subsection{Conclusion}
The UAP-01 spacecraft and UUAS simulation form a tightly integrated,
bidirectional scientific system. Spacecraft subsystems are designed
around simulation requirements, while simulation domains are validated
and refined by spacecraft telemetry. This coupling architecture enables
unprecedented multi-scale fidelity in the study of Uranus’s interior,
magnetic field, atmosphere, and ionosphere.